      "id": 1,
      "text": "Norwell got its name in 1888 when Henry Norwell, a dry‑goods merchant, funded road repairs and the town voted to honor him.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norwell%2C_Massachusetts"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "During the 18th and early 19th centuries, Norwell’s North River hosted shipyards that built frigates, schooners, whalers, and merchant vessels.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22,
        23,
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norwell%2C_Massachusetts"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The James Library, founded by Chicago resident Josiah Leavitt James, sits beside the First Parish Church and now serves as a community arts center.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        13,
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norwell%2C_Massachusetts"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "About a third of Norwell’s 10,000‑resident town is wetlands, preserving diverse wildlife along the North River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norwell%2C_Massachusetts"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Drivers can spot bass swimming at tide changes on the North River, and occasionally a seal sunbathes on its marshy banks.",
